(function(L){Object.assign(L.EXAM2_SOURCES,{"9.15":{"title":"9.15 What Is the History of the Grand Canyon_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.15","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Old rocks, younger gaps, much younger canyon\nBasement metamorphic rocks formed about 1.7 Ga, with somewhat younger granite. Uplift and erosion exposed this deep crust before later sediment accumulated.\nLate Precambrian strata around 1.1 Ga were deposited, tilted, and eroded. Younger Paleozoic strata lie across the resulting angular unconformity.\nContacts above crystalline basement may be nonconformities; contacts above tilted layered rocks may be angular unconformities. Parallel sedimentary intervals also contain disconformities.\nPaleozoic deposits record changing marine, river, and dune environments. Missing Ordovician and Silurian record and other gaps prevent treating the exposed column as continuous time.\nBracket the canyon separately\nThe chapter describes much later deformation and uplift, followed by major canyon excavation chiefly during the last roughly 5 million years. This is its reconstruction, not the age of the wall rocks.\nAn approximately 8 Ma plateau lava predates the main canyon; lava entering an existing canyon around 4–5 Ma shows the canyon already existed then. Faults also offset much younger basalt.\nThe canyon preserves only parts of Earth history: the earliest billions of years and long later intervals are absent. Rock thickness is not a uniform clock.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-15-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-15-figure.png","width":2274,"height":740,"alt":"Grand Canyon cross section with unconformities, basement, strata, and faults","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.15 · PDF page 1 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.0":{"title":"9.0 Chapter 9_ Geologic Time","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.0","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Siccar Point preserves steep older beds, erosion and younger deposits above them. The arrangement records multiple geological events.","image":"","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":null},"9.2":{"title":"9.2 How Do We Study Ages of Landscapes_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.2","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Build, expose, and cut\nSediment accumulates in successive layers, becomes rock, and may be uplifted as the sea withdraws. Streams then cut down; tributaries and slope failure widen a canyon.\nA canyon or erosion surface is younger than the rocks it cuts. A dated lava that covers the surface is younger than that surface, giving a second age limit.\nA river terrace records a former channel or floodplain level. An elevated abandoned terrace predates the river cutting to its present lower level.\nSurface clues and their limits\nSoil development can help compare surfaces under similar environmental conditions. Different climate, material, and disturbance histories complicate comparison.\nDesert varnish is a dark coating containing iron and manganese oxides; it can accumulate over long exposure but can also be stripped or reset.\nDesert pavement concentrates surface clasts as fine material is removed or moves downward. Well-developed pavement and varnish support prolonged exposure, not a universal stopwatch.\nCosmogenic isotopes accumulate through cosmic-ray interactions at exposed surfaces and can constrain exposure duration under an appropriate model.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-2-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-2-figure.png","width":984,"height":846,"alt":"Five-stage deposition, exposure, and canyon-development sequence","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.2 · PDF page 2 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.5":{"title":"9.5 What Are Fossils_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.5","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Ways the record is preserved\nHard shells, bones, and teeth commonly survive better than soft tissues. Mineral replacement can preserve form; silica replacement produces petrified wood.\nA mold is the cavity or impression left after an organism dissolves or disappears. A cast forms when that mold is filled.\nCarbon films preserve carbon-rich outlines; impressions preserve shapes. Amber can entomb small organisms. Stromatolites are layered structures built through microbial activity.\nTrace fossils, including footprints, trackways, and burrows, record activity rather than a preserved body. Trackways can reveal movement and behavior.\nPreservation is selective\nRapid burial and protection from scavenging, weathering, and oxygen favor preservation. Transport, compaction, and dissolution can destroy or alter remains; fossil absence is not proof that no life existed.\nPseudofossils resemble organisms but form without them. Branching manganese-oxide dendrites are mineral patterns. Concretions are localized cemented bodies and are not automatically fossils, although they may enclose one.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-5-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-5-figure.png","width":974,"height":610,"alt":"Original shell-mold fossil photograph","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.5 · PDF page 3 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.16":{"title":"9.16 What Is the Geologic History of This Place_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.16","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Read the field notes before ordering labels\nIn the supplied investigation, C is Cambrian beach sandstone/conglomerate, B is Ordovician marine shale, A is Permian terrestrial sandstone, S is Jurassic river/lake sandstone, and K is Cretaceous marine limestone. Fossils establish their relative sequence.\nGranite G is weathered beneath C, and C contains G clasts: G is older and the depositional contact is a nonconformity. The eroded parallel B–A contact is a disconformity.\nBasalt dike D bakes and cuts A, B, C, and G, so it is younger than those units. Fault F cuts several sedimentary units locally and in nearby exposures; include the field notes beyond the block view.\nRiver gravel R has a developed soil and middle-Cenozoic mammals. Its former channel is truncated by canyon N, and lava L enters that canyon: R deposition precedes canyon cutting, which precedes that lava.\nCompute ages under stated assumptions\nFor G, 125 parent atoms and 875 daughter atoms give a parent fraction of 1/8. Under the simple closed-system, zero-initial-daughter model, that is three 500-million-year half-lives, or 1,500 Ma.\nFor D, equal parent and daughter amounts give one half-life under the same model. With a 40-million-year half-life, the calculated age is 40 Ma.\nThese are instructional calculations, not measured analytical certainty. Separate numerical constraints, fossil ordering, and visible crosscutting evidence; do not infer an unsupported order merely from alphabetical labels.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-16-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-16-figure.png","width":1470,"height":1074,"alt":"Chapter investigation block with original G, A–C, D, F, K, S, R, N, V, L labels","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.16 · PDF page 3 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.7":{"title":"9.7 How Are Fossils Used to Infer Ages of Rocks_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.7","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Constrain an interval\nAn index fossil is especially useful when it is widespread, abundant, and restricted to a short time interval. A long-ranging fossil usually gives a weaker age limit.\nAn assemblage narrows an age to the overlap of fossil ranges. Do not take the union of every interval or average unrelated dates.\nMicrofossils can provide useful age markers. Abrupt changes in assemblages can help locate boundaries.\nMatch rocks without forcing a match\nCorrelate fossil assemblages alongside lithology and depositional environment. A missing species can reflect an unsuitable habitat rather than missing time.\nAn eroded unit may instead create a genuine gap. Distinguish environmental exclusion from erosion using contacts and the surrounding sequence.\nOverlapping partial sections can build a longer composite record. Equivalent units need not have identical thickness. William Smith used repeated fossil successions to correlate strata and map geology.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-7-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-7-figure.png","width":1420,"height":918,"alt":"Fossil-based correlation across two rock columns with missing intervals","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.7 · PDF page 4 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.11":{"title":"9.11 What Were Some Milestones in the Early History of Life on Earth_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.11","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"From microbial structures to marine diversity\nAncient stromatolites record microbial communities by about 3.5 billion years ago in the supplied examples. The oldest preserved evidence is not necessarily the exact origin of life.\nPhotosynthesis increased oxygen; ozone protection and changing cell organization helped create opportunities for later life. The source places sexual reproduction after about 1.5 billion years ago.\nThe Cambrian explosion, beginning around the course value of 541 Ma, was a geologically rapid diversification over millions of years, including many shelled organisms. It was not a single instantaneous event.\nEarly Paleozoic seas included trilobites, corals, crinoids, and mollusks. Fish and reefs expanded, followed by plants and insects on land and later amphibian and reptile diversification.\nThe end-Permian crisis\nThe Great Dying at about 252 Ma eliminated a large fraction of marine and terrestrial species. Its scale distinguishes it from ordinary background turnover.\nThe source discusses Siberian volcanism, environmental effects of Pangaea, ocean anoxia or circulation changes, and a disputed impact proposal. These are causal hypotheses with different support, not equally established facts.\nWhen reconstructing a crisis, separate dated coincidence, a plausible mechanism, and direct evidence. Do not turn a listed possibility into a confirmed sole cause.","image":"","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":null},"9.1":{"title":"9.1 How Do We Infer the Relative Ages of Events_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.1","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Five relative-age tools\nMost sedimentary beds and many lava layers begin approximately horizontal: original horizontality. Dune foresets and delta slopes are exceptions; tilting or folding generally records later deformation.\nSuperposition places younger beds above older beds in an undisturbed sequence. First check whether the beds have been overturned.\nInclusions are older than the rock that contains them. A granite fragment inside conglomerate existed before the conglomerate formed.\nCrosscutting relationships make the cutting fault, vein, or dike younger than the material it cuts. A feature that stops at a contact need not cut the next unit.\nBaked contacts identify preexisting rock heated by younger magma. A sill can bake rock above and below; a lava flow can bake underlying rock while later sediment deposited above remains unbaked.\nDeep time from ordinary processes\nSiccar Point combines steep older beds, erosion, and gently dipping younger beds. Deposition, lithification, deformation, and erosion require a sequence; the present arrangement was not deposited all at once.\nUniformitarianism uses processes observable today to interpret earlier events. It does not require every process to operate at an unchanging rate.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-1-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-1-figure.png","width":972,"height":690,"alt":"Baked rock beside an original basalt dike photograph","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.1 · PDF page 7 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.8":{"title":"9.8 How Was the Geologic Timescale Developed_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.8","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Relative framework, then numerical calibration\nThe timescale developed from superposition, fossil succession, and correlation before numerical ages were available. Isotope dates later calibrated that framework.\nThe three Phanerozoic eras are Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic. “Precambrian” is a broad earlier span, not a fourth era equivalent to those three.\nPaleozoic periods in the supplied course sequence: Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian, Permian. Mississippian and Pennsylvanian together correspond to the Carboniferous used elsewhere.\nMesozoic periods: Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous. Cenozoic periods: Paleogene, Neogene, Quaternary. Major extinction events mark some boundaries.\nBracket events with dated rocks\nAn interbedded ash can closely date a depositional horizon. Dated volcanic layers below and above a fossil bed bracket its age.\nA dike is younger than beds it cuts; clasts eroded from that dike are older than a later deposit containing them. Neither relationship makes the sediment exactly the dike age.\nAn unconformity is younger than the underlying record and older than overlying deposition. Use source boundary values as course approximations rather than exact natural switches.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-8-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-8-figure.png","width":1474,"height":1600,"alt":"Original course geologic timescale with eras, periods, and boundary dates","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.8 · PDF page 2 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.13":{"title":"9.13 How Do We Reconstruct Geologic Histories_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.13","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Correlate with independent evidence\nTraceable lateral continuity is strong evidence that separated exposures belong to one unit. Distinctive beds, repeated sequences, shared events, and regional unconformities also support correlation.\nFacies can change sideways: a sandy beach and offshore mud may form at the same time. Equal age does not require identical lithology or thickness.\nFossil assemblages, suitable isotope ages, and paleomagnetic reversal patterns add constraints. A matching pattern suggests correlation but must be evaluated with the rest of the evidence.\nBuild a sequence, not a guess\nMissing units may result from nondeposition, environmental restriction, thickness changes, or erosion. Inspect contacts to distinguish these alternatives.\nThe supplied block combines old metamorphic basement, granite, unconformities, sedimentary beds, a dike and lava, fault motion, canyon cutting, and river gravel. Apply each visible relationship to a pair of events first.\nA fault can move more than once. A preexisting scarp and later displacement of lava can represent different episodes; “the fault” need not be one instant.\nKeep an event uncertain if no observed relation orders it. A complete-looking list is less useful than an honest set of supported constraints.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-13-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-13-figure.png","width":1216,"height":836,"alt":"Numbered geological-history block with contacts, fault, dike, and canyon","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.13 · PDF page 6 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.6":{"title":"9.6 How and Why Did Living Things Change Through Geologic Time_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.6","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"The broad sequence\nFaunal succession is the systematic change of fossil assemblages through rock sequences. This relative pattern was recognized before isotope dating supplied numerical ages.\nPrecambrian time comprises roughly 90% of Earth history; much of its record is dominated by simple life and microbial structures.\nThe Paleozoic records major marine diversification and the expansion of plants and animals onto land. The Mesozoic includes dinosaur-dominated terrestrial ecosystems; the Cenozoic records extensive mammal diversification.\nCourse boundary values are about 541, 252, and 66 million years ago. Broad labels summarize change; they do not mean that all members of a group originated or vanished simultaneously.\nWhy populations change\nEnvironmental setting, climate, resources, reproduction, adaptability, competition, and predator–prey relationships influence survival and reproduction.\nNatural selection explains changes in populations through differences in survival and reproductive success. Evolutionary change is an observation; mechanisms explain how it occurs.\nGradual change and geologically rapid episodes separated by relative stability can both occur. Punctuated equilibrium does not mean an individual suddenly transforms into a new species.","image":"","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":null},"9.4":{"title":"9.4 How Are Ages Assigned to Rocks and Events_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.4","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Parent, daughter, and half-life\nIsotopes of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Unstable parent isotopes decay into daughter products.\nA half-life removes half of the remaining parent: 100% → 50% → 25% → 12.5%. Equal time intervals remove successively smaller amounts, not a fixed number of atoms.\nParent/daughter ratios can give an age when the initial daughter contribution and later gain or loss are addressed. A mass spectrometer measures isotope proportions; each result has analytical uncertainty.\nChoose the material and the event\nK–Ar and Ar–Ar commonly date volcanic eruption or cooling; U–Pb in zircon commonly dates crystallization. Rb–Sr and Sm–Nd are useful for suitable older rocks and mineral systems.\nCarbon-14 is useful for comparatively recent carbon-bearing material such as charcoal or bone, not for dating a billion-year-old granite.\nA slowly cooled granite may record zircon crystallization and later biotite cooling through about 300°C at different times. Different mineral ages need not be contradictory.\nDetrital mineral grains can date their source rocks rather than sediment deposition. New metamorphic minerals or reset systems may date metamorphism. Always name the event being measured.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-4-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-4-figure.png","width":1448,"height":444,"alt":"Original parent and daughter atom arrays through two half-lives","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.4 · PDF page 2 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.10":{"title":"9.10 What Events Occurred Early in Eart...ry and How Did Earth Change Over Time_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.10","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Impacts and a hot planet\nAn impact converts kinetic energy into shock, fracture, heating, and sometimes melting. Excavation ejects material; a smaller crater may remain simple, whereas larger complex craters can develop central rebound peaks.\nEarly Earth retained formation heat, experienced more impacts, and contained more heat-producing radioactive isotopes. Archean ultramafic lavas and greenstone belts record distinctive early conditions.\nThe onset and character of early plate tectonics remain uncertain in the supplied discussion; do not infer a precise start date from a single rock.\nAtmosphere, water, and free oxygen\nLight early gases escaped; volcanic outgassing supplied water vapor, carbon dioxide, and sulfur-bearing gases. Cooling and water accumulation helped establish oceans.\nPhotosynthetic microbes produced free oxygen. Oxygen combined with dissolved iron to form iron-rich deposits; banded iron formations alternate iron-rich layers with silica-rich material.\nThe course places the Great Oxygenation Event around 2.3–2.4 billion years ago, followed by further changes. Oxygen in minerals is not the same as free oxygen gas in the atmosphere.\nPreserved oxygen-sensitive minerals and later oxidized red beds help reconstruct atmospheric change. The detailed oxygenation history remains an active scientific problem.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-10-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-10-figure.png","width":1448,"height":280,"alt":"Original impact, excavation, and simple/complex crater sequence","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.10 · PDF page 1 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.12":{"title":"9.12 What Were Some Milestones in the Later History of Life on Earth_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.12","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Life before the end-Cretaceous boundary\nTriassic ecosystems included early mammals, dinosaur relatives, conifers, and marine ammonites. Mammals did not originate only after dinosaurs disappeared.\nJurassic ecosystems included diverse dinosaurs and early birds such as Archaeopteryx. Cretaceous ecosystems included flowering plants, birds, mammals, marine reptiles, fish, and plankton.\nDinosaur hip categories describe anatomy; a group name alone does not establish bird ancestry. Read the relationship described by the source instead of treating a label as a family tree.\nExtinction and later diversification\nAt about 66 Ma the end-Cretaceous crisis eliminated nonavian dinosaurs and many other groups. The Chicxulub impact in Mexico supplied dust and gases capable of disrupting sunlight and food webs.\nThe chapter also discusses Deccan volcanism as a possible contributing influence. Distinguish an impact mechanism from an unsupported claim that every extinction had one identical cause.\nMammals diversified extensively in the Cenozoic. Later ice-age ecosystems included large mammals; climate and other changing conditions are relevant to their losses.\nOrigin, diversification, and extinction are separate milestones. A group can exist long before it becomes ecologically prominent.","image":"","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":null},"9.14":{"title":"9.14 Why Do We Investigate Geologic History_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.14","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Hazards from a longer record\nLava superposition, weathering, and suitable isotopic dates reconstruct eruption history. This constrains hazards without predicting the exact date of the next eruption.\nOffset dated rocks constrain fault activity; a lava covering a scarp can limit older movement. Scarp preservation also depends on erosion.\nFlood gauges often cover a much shorter interval than deposits, terraces, soils, and datable charcoal. Geological evidence extends the history beyond human measurements.\nResources and human history\nIntrusions and crosscutting hydrothermal veins help reconstruct mineralization. The timing of folding, burial, migration, and erosion matters to oil and gas traps.\nBasin stratigraphy controls groundwater pathways. An isotope-based water residence time is a different measurement from the age of the surrounding sediment.\nArchaeology combines layer order, construction relationships, tree rings, and appropriate radiocarbon dates. Reused building stones can predate the wall containing them.\nAncient hominid deposits can be bracketed by volcanic ash; carbon-14 is not a suitable clock for material millions of years old.","image":"","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":null},"9.3":{"title":"9.3 What Is the Significance of an Unconformity_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.3","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Recognize the contact\nAn unconformity is a surface representing missing geologic record through erosion, nondeposition, or both. The gap is time, not necessarily a visibly open space.\nAn angular unconformity separates older tilted or folded strata from younger beds at a different orientation. Sequence: deposition → deformation → erosion → renewed deposition.\nA nonconformity places sedimentary rock on an eroded surface of nonlayered crystalline rock, such as granite. The granite formed at depth before uplift and erosion exposed it.\nA disconformity separates essentially parallel sedimentary beds. Fossils, weathering, or erosional relief may reveal a gap that bedding orientation alone hides.\nDo not skip the missing events\nErosion can truncate layers and remove a long record. Nondeposition can leave missing time even where little material was removed.\nThe supplied chapter identifies angular contacts over steeply layered metamorphic rocks as well as tilted sedimentary beds. Inspect the geometry and rock character; do not classify from the word “metamorphic” alone.","image":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-3-figure.png","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":{"kind":"img","src":"assets/figures/ch9-lessons/selected/9-3-figure.png","width":1454,"height":326,"alt":"Angular unconformity: deposition, folding and erosion, renewed deposition","cap":"Exploring Geology · 9.3 · PDF page 2 · original figure extracted from the supplied page"}},"9.9":{"title":"9.9 What Is the Evidence for the Age of Earth_","citation":"Exploring Geology · Chapter 9 · 9.9","pages":[{"n":1,"text":"Why early estimates were incomplete\nAn early ocean-salt estimate assumed salt only accumulated; it neglected removal into deposits. An early cooling estimate omitted internal radioactive heat. Both illustrate how a missing process biases a model.\nErosion, annual ice layers, seasonal sediment varves, and slow plate motion independently demonstrate long durations, but no single one must preserve all of Earth history.\nOld material and converging clocks\nThe supplied chapter uses an Earth age of about 4.55 billion years. Meteorites around 4.53–4.58 billion years and ancient lunar material support early solar-system formation ages.\nAbout 4.0-billion-year-old Acasta gneiss preserves an older igneous history altered by metamorphism. Some detrital zircons exceed 4.4 billion years; their host sediment is younger.\nEarth recycles crust through erosion, melting, metamorphism, and plate processes. Its oldest surviving rock need not equal its formation age.\nAgreement among suitable independent isotope systems and coherent regional age patterns strengthens the interpretation. An individual mineral age still requires an event and material context.","image":"","blank":false}],"selectedFigure":null}});})(window.L);

