var lessons=[{"n": 1, "title": "Relative ages: read the relationship", "pages": [2, 7], "groups": [["Five relative-age tools", ["Most sedimentary beds and many lava layers begin approximately horizontal: <term>original horizontality</term>. Dune foresets and delta slopes are exceptions; tilting or folding generally records later deformation.", "<term>Superposition</term> places younger beds above older beds in an undisturbed sequence. First check whether the beds have been overturned.", "<term>Inclusions</term> are older than the rock that contains them. A granite fragment inside conglomerate existed before the conglomerate formed.", "<term>Crosscutting relationships</term> make the cutting fault, vein, or dike younger than the material it cuts. A feature that stops at a contact need not cut the next unit.", "<term>Baked contacts</term> identify preexisting rock heated by younger magma. A sill can bake rock above and below; a lava flow can bake underlying rock while later sediment deposited above remains unbaked."]], ["Deep time from ordinary processes", ["Siccar Point combines steep older beds, erosion, and gently dipping younger beds. Deposition, lithification, deformation, and erosion require a sequence; the present arrangement was not deposited all at once.", "<term>Uniformitarianism</term> uses processes observable today to interpret earlier events. It does not require every process to operate at an unchanging rate."]]], "questions": [["A conglomerate contains rounded granite pebbles. What relationship follows?", ["The granite formed before the conglomerate", "The conglomerate formed before the granite", "The rocks must have identical ages", "The granite intruded the conglomerate"], "The pebbles had to exist before erosion and incorporation into the younger deposit.", ["The granite clasts had to exist before the conglomerate could incorporate them.", "Incorporation establishes an older-younger relation, not equal formation ages.", "Rounded clasts indicate transported fragments; the question does not describe an intrusive granite contact."]], ["A tabular igneous body has baked sedimentary rock along both its upper and lower contacts. Which interpretation fits?", ["A sill intruded existing sedimentary layers", "A lava flow cooled before upper sediment arrived", "A fault juxtaposed two unheated units", "A sedimentary bed became overturned"], "Heating on both sides supports intrusion into already present rock.", ["A cooled surface lava cannot bake sediment that arrives afterward.", "Juxtaposition alone does not explain thermal alteration along both contacts.", "Overturning changes orientation but does not supply the heat that baked the contacts."]]], "writing": ["Explain why a lava flow and a sill can have different effects on the rock above them.", "A sill intrudes preexisting rock and can bake it above and below. Sediment deposited after a lava flow cools is younger and is not baked by that eruption."], "rubric": ["States that a sill intrudes rock already present above and below it.", "Explains that sill heat can bake both contacts.", "States that sediment deposited after lava has cooled is younger and is not baked by that eruption."]}, {"n": 2, "title": "Landscape age is not rock age", "pages": [2, 5], "groups": [["Build, expose, and cut", ["Sediment accumulates in successive layers, becomes rock, and may be uplifted as the sea withdraws. Streams then cut down; tributaries and slope failure widen a canyon.", "A canyon or erosion surface is <term>younger than the rocks it cuts</term>. A dated lava that covers the surface is younger than that surface, giving a second age limit.", "A river terrace records a former channel or floodplain level. An elevated abandoned terrace predates the river cutting to its present lower level."]], ["Surface clues and their limits", ["Soil development can help compare surfaces under similar environmental conditions. Different climate, material, and disturbance histories complicate comparison.", "<term>Desert varnish</term> is a dark coating containing iron and manganese oxides; it can accumulate over long exposure but can also be stripped or reset.", "<term>Desert pavement</term> concentrates surface clasts as fine material is removed or moves downward. Well-developed pavement and varnish support prolonged exposure, not a universal stopwatch.", "<term>Cosmogenic isotopes</term> accumulate through cosmic-ray interactions at exposed surfaces and can constrain exposure duration under an appropriate model."]]], "questions": [["An erosion surface cuts 80-million-year-old rock and is covered by a 12-million-year-old lava. Which age fits the surface?", ["30 million years", "100 million years", "5 million years", "Exactly 80 million years"], "The surface formed after the older rock but before the younger covering lava.", ["100 Ma predates the 80 Ma rock that the surface cuts.", "5 Ma postdates the 12 Ma lava that already covers the surface.", "The rock formed before erosion cut its surface; its formation date is not automatically the surface date."]], ["Two nearby terraces have similar parent material and climate. Which observation most supports longer exposure of one terrace?", ["A more developed undisturbed soil", "A darker bedrock unrelated to soil development", "A thinner soil stripped by recent erosion", "A fresher coating exposed by recent disturbance"], "Comparable soils can indicate relative surface exposure; a single unrelated property cannot.", ["Bedrock color can reflect mineral composition rather than time spent forming a soil.", "Recent stripping removes soil development and can make an old surface appear young.", "A newly exposed coating records disturbance, not a long undisturbed soil history."]]], "writing": ["Explain why dating a canyon wall does not directly date canyon excavation.", "The wall rock formed first. Erosion later cut the canyon through it, so the rock age supplies an older limit rather than the excavation date."], "rubric": ["States that wall rock formed before the canyon cut through it.", "Distinguishes rock formation age from the later excavation event.", "Uses the wall-rock date as an older limit, not the exact canyon age."]}, {"n": 3, "title": "Unconformities: classify missing time", "pages": [1, 6], "groups": [["Recognize the contact", ["An <term>unconformity</term> is a surface representing missing geologic record through erosion, nondeposition, or both. The gap is time, not necessarily a visibly open space.", "An <term>angular unconformity</term> separates older tilted or folded strata from younger beds at a different orientation. Sequence: deposition → deformation → erosion → renewed deposition.", "A <term>nonconformity</term> places sedimentary rock on an eroded surface of nonlayered crystalline rock, such as granite. The granite formed at depth before uplift and erosion exposed it.", "A <term>disconformity</term> separates essentially parallel sedimentary beds. Fossils, weathering, or erosional relief may reveal a gap that bedding orientation alone hides."]], ["Do not skip the missing events", ["Erosion can truncate layers and remove a long record. Nondeposition can leave missing time even where little material was removed.", "The supplied chapter identifies angular contacts over steeply layered metamorphic rocks as well as tilted sedimentary beds. Inspect the geometry and rock character; do not classify from the word “metamorphic” alone."]]], "questions": [["Horizontal sandstone overlies truncated, tilted limestone beds. Which contact is present?", ["Angular unconformity", "Disconformity", "Nonconformity", "Conformable contact"], "The changed orientation and truncated older beds identify an angular unconformity.", ["A disconformity has essentially parallel beds, unlike the tilted and horizontal beds described.", "A nonconformity involves sediment deposited on crystalline basement, not the limestone beds described.", "Truncation and changed bedding orientation require intervening deformation and erosion."]], ["Parallel marine beds contain a fossil-age gap and an eroded upper surface on the lower bed. Which contact fits?", ["Disconformity", "Angular unconformity", "Intrusive contact", "Nonconformity"], "Parallel sedimentary beds can still contain an erosional hiatus.", ["The beds remain parallel; a change in bedding angle is not given.", "No magma or baked contact is described; the evidence is an eroded sedimentary surface.", "Both sides are sedimentary beds rather than sediment above nonlayered crystalline basement."]]], "writing": ["Describe how sandstone can come to rest on granite across a nonconformity.", "Granite crystallizes at depth, uplift and erosion expose it, its surface is weathered or eroded, and younger sand is deposited and lithified above it."], "rubric": ["Begins with granite crystallizing at depth.", "Includes uplift and erosion exposing the granite surface.", "Ends with younger sand deposition and lithification above the eroded granite."]}, {"n": 4, "title": "Numerical ages and what the clock dates", "pages": [2, 5], "groups": [["Parent, daughter, and half-life", ["<term>Isotopes</term> of an element have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons. Unstable parent isotopes decay into daughter products.", "A <term>half-life</term> removes half of the remaining parent: 100% → 50% → 25% → 12.5%. Equal time intervals remove successively smaller amounts, not a fixed number of atoms.", "Parent/daughter ratios can give an age when the initial daughter contribution and later gain or loss are addressed. A mass spectrometer measures isotope proportions; each result has analytical uncertainty."]], ["Choose the material and the event", ["K–Ar and Ar–Ar commonly date volcanic eruption or cooling; U–Pb in zircon commonly dates crystallization. Rb–Sr and Sm–Nd are useful for suitable older rocks and mineral systems.", "<term>Carbon-14</term> is useful for comparatively recent carbon-bearing material such as charcoal or bone, not for dating a billion-year-old granite.", "A slowly cooled granite may record zircon crystallization and later biotite cooling through about 300°C at different times. Different mineral ages need not be contradictory.", "Detrital mineral grains can date their source rocks rather than sediment deposition. New metamorphic minerals or reset systems may date metamorphism. Always name the event being measured."]]], "questions": [["A closed mineral initially contained only parent isotope. It now retains one-eighth of its original parent. How many half-lives passed?", ["Three", "One", "Two", "Eight"], "One-half, one-quarter, and one-eighth correspond to one, two, and three half-lives.", ["One half-life leaves one-half of the parent, not one-eighth.", "Two half-lives leave one-quarter of the parent, not one-eighth.", "Eight is the inverse fraction; repeated halving reaches one-eighth after three intervals."]], ["A sandstone contains zircon grains that crystallized 900 million years ago. What does that date directly describe?", ["Crystallization of the detrital zircon", "Deposition of every sandstone bed", "The most recent erosion of the outcrop", "The age of all fossils in the sandstone"], "Inherited grains formed before their incorporation into sediment.", ["The zircon existed before it was deposited as a detrital grain.", "The date measures isotope-system history, not the most recent exposure or erosion event.", "A source-grain age does not assign an age to younger fossils deposited with the sediment."]]], "writing": ["Why might zircon and biotite in one granite give different valid ages?", "They can record different events or closure conditions: zircon may date crystallization while biotite records later cooling. The mineral system and thermal history must be considered."], "rubric": ["Identifies zircon age as potentially recording crystallization.", "Identifies biotite age as potentially recording later cooling through its closure conditions.", "Explains that differing recorded events can yield different valid ages in one rock."]}, {"n": 5, "title": "Fossils: remains, impressions, and traces", "pages": [2, 7], "groups": [["Ways the record is preserved", ["Hard shells, bones, and teeth commonly survive better than soft tissues. Mineral replacement can preserve form; silica replacement produces petrified wood.", "A <term>mold</term> is the cavity or impression left after an organism dissolves or disappears. A <term>cast</term> forms when that mold is filled.", "Carbon films preserve carbon-rich outlines; impressions preserve shapes. Amber can entomb small organisms. <term>Stromatolites</term> are layered structures built through microbial activity.", "<term>Trace fossils</term>, including footprints, trackways, and burrows, record activity rather than a preserved body. Trackways can reveal movement and behavior."]], ["Preservation is selective", ["Rapid burial and protection from scavenging, weathering, and oxygen favor preservation. Transport, compaction, and dissolution can destroy or alter remains; fossil absence is not proof that no life existed.", "<term>Pseudofossils</term> resemble organisms but form without them. Branching manganese-oxide dendrites are mineral patterns. Concretions are localized cemented bodies and are not automatically fossils, although they may enclose one."]]], "questions": [["A shell dissolves, leaving a cavity that is later filled by sediment. What is the filled replica?", ["Cast", "Mold", "Carbon film", "Dendrite"], "The cavity is the mold; its later fill produces a cast.", ["A mold is the unfilled cavity; the question asks about its later fill.", "A carbon film is a thin residue rather than a filled shell cavity.", "A dendrite is a branching mineral pattern rather than a replica formed by filling a mold."]], ["Which specimen most directly records an organism’s behavior rather than its body?", ["A sequence of footprints", "A mineral-replaced tooth", "A shell fragment", "A compressed leaf film"], "A trackway records movement and is a trace fossil.", ["A tooth preserves part of the organism’s body rather than its movement.", "A shell fragment is a body remain rather than evidence of locomotion.", "A leaf film preserves the body outline rather than a trail of behavior."]]], "writing": ["Explain why a rock without fossils does not prove a lifeless original setting.", "Organisms may have lacked durable parts, failed to be buried rapidly, or been destroyed by decay, transport, dissolution, or later alteration. Preservation is selective."], "rubric": ["Identifies at least one preservation filter such as lack of durable parts, slow burial, decay, transport, or dissolution.", "Explains how that filter can remove evidence of organisms that were present.", "Concludes that fossil absence alone does not establish an originally lifeless setting."]}, {"n": 6, "title": "Changing life and faunal succession", "pages": [2, 8], "groups": [["The broad sequence", ["<term>Faunal succession</term> is the systematic change of fossil assemblages through rock sequences. This relative pattern was recognized before isotope dating supplied numerical ages.", "Precambrian time comprises roughly 90% of Earth history; much of its record is dominated by simple life and microbial structures.", "The <term>Paleozoic</term> records major marine diversification and the expansion of plants and animals onto land. The <term>Mesozoic</term> includes dinosaur-dominated terrestrial ecosystems; the <term>Cenozoic</term> records extensive mammal diversification.", "Course boundary values are about 541, 252, and 66 million years ago. Broad labels summarize change; they do not mean that all members of a group originated or vanished simultaneously."]], ["Why populations change", ["Environmental setting, climate, resources, reproduction, adaptability, competition, and predator–prey relationships influence survival and reproduction.", "<term>Natural selection</term> explains changes in populations through differences in survival and reproductive success. Evolutionary change is an observation; mechanisms explain how it occurs.", "Gradual change and geologically rapid episodes separated by relative stability can both occur. <term>Punctuated equilibrium</term> does not mean an individual suddenly transforms into a new species."]]], "questions": [["A repeated sequence of fossil assemblages appears in several undisturbed rock columns. Which principle supports relative correlation?", ["Faunal succession", "Radioactive half-life", "Contact metamorphism", "Original horizontality"], "Successive characteristic assemblages establish relative ordering across sections.", ["Half-life is a numerical isotope clock, not the principle behind an ordered succession of fossil assemblages.", "Contact metamorphism records heating beside an intrusion, not a regional fossil sequence.", "Original horizontality concerns initial bedding attitude, not which assemblage follows another."]], ["A climatic shift favors a heritable trait that improves reproductive success. Which mechanism best explains its increasing frequency?", ["Natural selection", "Inheritance of every change acquired during life", "Identical reproductive success for every trait", "Directional change unrelated to reproductive success"], "Selection acts through differential survival and reproduction in an environment.", ["Acquired changes during life are not automatically inherited; the prompt specifies heritable differences.", "Selection in this scenario depends on unequal reproductive success, not identical success.", "The stated advantage affects reproductive success, which supplies the mechanism for increasing frequency."]]], "writing": ["Why is “mammals diversified in the Cenozoic” different from “mammals first appeared in the Cenozoic”?", "Diversification means expansion in forms and ecological roles. Mammals already existed in the Mesozoic, so a later diversification does not mark their origin."], "rubric": ["States that mammals existed before the Cenozoic.", "Defines diversification as expansion in forms or ecological roles.", "Distinguishes this later expansion from the first appearance of mammals."]}, {"n": 7, "title": "Fossil ranges and correlation", "pages": [2, 5], "groups": [["Constrain an interval", ["An <term>index fossil</term> is especially useful when it is widespread, abundant, and restricted to a short time interval. A long-ranging fossil usually gives a weaker age limit.", "An assemblage narrows an age to the <term>overlap of fossil ranges</term>. Do not take the union of every interval or average unrelated dates.", "Microfossils can provide useful age markers. Abrupt changes in assemblages can help locate boundaries."]], ["Match rocks without forcing a match", ["Correlate fossil assemblages alongside lithology and depositional environment. A missing species can reflect an unsuitable habitat rather than missing time.", "An eroded unit may instead create a genuine gap. Distinguish environmental exclusion from erosion using contacts and the surrounding sequence.", "Overlapping partial sections can build a longer composite record. Equivalent units need not have identical thickness. William Smith used repeated fossil successions to correlate strata and map geology."]]], "questions": [["Fossil A lived from 120 to 100 Ma; fossil B from 110 to 90 Ma. What interval fits a bed containing both?", ["110–100 Ma", "120–90 Ma", "120–110 Ma", "100–90 Ma"], "The shared interval is the intersection, 110–100 Ma.", ["120–90 Ma combines both entire ranges instead of retaining only their overlap.", "Before 110 Ma, fossil B had not yet appeared.", "After 100 Ma, fossil A was no longer present."]], ["Which organism would generally be the strongest index fossil?", ["An abundant widespread species with a short range", "A rare local species with a long range", "A widespread species spanning most geologic time", "A species known only from one restricted habitat"], "Short duration narrows age; widespread abundance enables correlation.", ["A long range limits precision, while rarity and local occurrence limit correlation.", "An extremely long-lived species gives a broad interval rather than a narrow age constraint.", "Restriction to one habitat can prevent widespread correlation even if the species is distinctive."]]], "writing": ["Give two different reasons a fossil-bearing bed might be absent in another section.", "The environment may not have supported or preserved that assemblage, or the relevant rock interval may have been removed by erosion or never deposited."], "rubric": ["Gives environmental exclusion or failure of preservation as one reason.", "Gives erosion or nondeposition of the interval as a different reason.", "Distinguishes absence of a fossil from absence of the entire rock interval."]}, {"n": 8, "title": "Build and calibrate the geologic timescale", "pages": [1, 5], "groups": [["Relative framework, then numerical calibration", ["The timescale developed from superposition, fossil succession, and correlation before numerical ages were available. Isotope dates later calibrated that framework.", "The three Phanerozoic eras are <term>Paleozoic → Mesozoic → Cenozoic</term>. “Precambrian” is a broad earlier span, not a fourth era equivalent to those three.", "Paleozoic periods in the supplied course sequence: <term>Cambrian, Ordovician, Silurian, Devonian, Mississippian, Pennsylvanian, Permian</term>. Mississippian and Pennsylvanian together correspond to the Carboniferous used elsewhere.", "Mesozoic periods: <term>Triassic, Jurassic, Cretaceous</term>. Cenozoic periods: <term>Paleogene, Neogene, Quaternary</term>. Major extinction events mark some boundaries."]], ["Bracket events with dated rocks", ["An interbedded ash can closely date a depositional horizon. Dated volcanic layers below and above a fossil bed bracket its age.", "A dike is younger than beds it cuts; clasts eroded from that dike are older than a later deposit containing them. Neither relationship makes the sediment exactly the dike age.", "An unconformity is younger than the underlying record and older than overlying deposition. Use source boundary values as course approximations rather than exact natural switches."]]], "questions": [["A fossil bed lies above a 105 Ma ash and below a 98 Ma ash. Which age is consistent?", ["101 Ma", "110 Ma", "95 Ma", "150 Ma"], "Superposition brackets the bed between the two ash ages.", ["110 Ma is older than the underlying 105 Ma ash.", "95 Ma is younger than the overlying 98 Ma ash.", "150 Ma is older than both bracketing ash layers."]], ["Which sequence lists the Mesozoic periods from oldest to youngest?", ["Triassic → Jurassic → Cretaceous", "Jurassic → Triassic → Cretaceous", "Cretaceous → Jurassic → Triassic", "Triassic → Cretaceous → Jurassic"], "Triassic precedes Jurassic, which precedes Cretaceous.", ["Triassic precedes Jurassic, so these first two periods are reversed.", "This lists the three periods from youngest to oldest.", "Jurassic lies between Triassic and Cretaceous, not after Cretaceous."]]], "writing": ["How could geologists construct a relative timescale before isotope dating existed?", "They used stratigraphic order, fossil succession, and correlation to establish the sequence. Isotope dating later assigned numerical ages to parts of that relative framework."], "rubric": ["Identifies stratigraphic order or superposition as relative-age evidence.", "Identifies fossil succession and correlation as tools linking different sections.", "States that isotope dating later calibrated this ordered framework with numerical ages."]}, {"n": 9, "title": "Evidence for Earth’s age", "pages": [1, 7], "groups": [["Why early estimates were incomplete", ["An early ocean-salt estimate assumed salt only accumulated; it neglected removal into deposits. An early cooling estimate omitted internal radioactive heat. Both illustrate how a missing process biases a model.", "Erosion, annual ice layers, seasonal sediment <term>varves</term>, and slow plate motion independently demonstrate long durations, but no single one must preserve all of Earth history."]], ["Old material and converging clocks", ["The supplied chapter uses an Earth age of about <term>4.55 billion years</term>. Meteorites around 4.53–4.58 billion years and ancient lunar material support early solar-system formation ages.", "About 4.0-billion-year-old Acasta gneiss preserves an older igneous history altered by metamorphism. Some detrital zircons exceed 4.4 billion years; their host sediment is younger.", "Earth recycles crust through erosion, melting, metamorphism, and plate processes. Its oldest surviving rock need not equal its formation age.", "Agreement among suitable independent isotope systems and coherent regional age patterns strengthens the interpretation. An individual mineral age still requires an event and material context."]]], "questions": [["Why did an ocean-salt accumulation estimate underestimate Earth’s age?", ["It neglected processes removing salt from seawater", "It counted salt removal twice in the budget", "It treated ocean salt as already present initially", "It assumed rivers supplied salt much more slowly"], "Ignoring salt removal makes today’s inventory look like a shorter accumulation history.", ["The historical estimate omitted removal rather than subtracting the loss twice.", "The stated problem is an omitted sink, not an initial salt inventory.", "Assuming slower river supply would tend to lengthen an accumulation estimate; it is not the omitted process discussed."]], ["Why can Earth be older than its oldest known intact rock?", ["Earlier rocks were recycled or destroyed", "The oldest rock marks the exact start of accretion", "Every surviving rock stayed unchanged since formation", "A younger metamorphic age resets the planet’s age"], "Survival of a rock is different from formation of the planet.", ["The oldest surviving rock only establishes that Earth already existed by then.", "Earth’s recycling destroys or alters older material, so surviving rocks need not remain unchanged.", "Metamorphism can reset a rock clock without restarting the planet’s formation history."]]], "writing": ["Distinguish the age of an ancient detrital zircon from the age of its sedimentary host.", "The zircon crystallized in a source rock before weathering, transport, and deposition. Its age records that earlier crystallization, not directly the younger sediment deposition."], "rubric": ["States that the zircon crystallized in an older source rock.", "Places weathering, transport, and sediment deposition after zircon crystallization.", "Explains that the grain age does not directly date deposition of its host sediment."]}, {"n": 10, "title": "Early Earth: impacts, heat, air, and oceans", "pages": [1, 4], "groups": [["Impacts and a hot planet", ["An impact converts kinetic energy into shock, fracture, heating, and sometimes melting. Excavation ejects material; a smaller crater may remain simple, whereas larger complex craters can develop central rebound peaks.", "Early Earth retained formation heat, experienced more impacts, and contained more heat-producing radioactive isotopes. Archean ultramafic lavas and greenstone belts record distinctive early conditions.", "The onset and character of early plate tectonics remain uncertain in the supplied discussion; do not infer a precise start date from a single rock."]], ["Atmosphere, water, and free oxygen", ["Light early gases escaped; volcanic outgassing supplied water vapor, carbon dioxide, and sulfur-bearing gases. Cooling and water accumulation helped establish oceans.", "Photosynthetic microbes produced <term>free oxygen</term>. Oxygen combined with dissolved iron to form iron-rich deposits; banded iron formations alternate iron-rich layers with silica-rich material.", "The course places the <term>Great Oxygenation Event</term> around 2.3–2.4 billion years ago, followed by further changes. Oxygen in minerals is not the same as free oxygen gas in the atmosphere.", "Preserved oxygen-sensitive minerals and later oxidized red beds help reconstruct atmospheric change. The detailed oxygenation history remains an active scientific problem."]]], "questions": [["Which sequence best describes impact-crater development?", ["Shock and fracture → excavation and ejecta → final crater", "Excavation and ejecta → shock → final crater", "Final crater → shock → excavation and ejecta", "Shock and fracture → final crater → incoming impact"], "Impact shock precedes excavation and final crater adjustment.", ["Shock begins on impact before excavation throws material outward.", "The final crater develops after shock and excavation, not before them.", "The incoming impact initiates the sequence rather than following crater formation."]], ["What most directly links photosynthetic microbes to banded iron formation?", ["Their oxygen promoted oxidation and precipitation of dissolved iron", "They supplied shell fragments that became all iron layers", "They depleted oxygen and prevented iron oxidation", "They dated the layers through parent-isotope decay"], "Biologically produced oxygen reacted with dissolved iron in the ocean.", ["Banded iron is tied to chemical precipitation of dissolved iron, not mechanical accumulation of microbial shells.", "Oxygen production enables iron oxidation; preventing oxidation reverses that relationship.", "Isotope dating can constrain an age but does not explain why iron precipitated."]]], "writing": ["Why is oxygen within ancient minerals not proof of an oxygen-rich ancient atmosphere?", "Chemically bound oxygen in minerals differs from free molecular oxygen in air. Atmospheric reconstruction requires evidence sensitive to free oxygen, such as oxidation state and preserved minerals."], "rubric": ["Distinguishes chemically bound oxygen in minerals from free molecular oxygen in air.", "Explains why the first does not directly establish the abundance of the second.", "Names an atmospheric indicator such as oxygen-sensitive minerals or evidence of oxidation."]}, {"n": 11, "title": "Early milestones in life", "pages": [2, 6], "groups": [["From microbial structures to marine diversity", ["Ancient stromatolites record microbial communities by about 3.5 billion years ago in the supplied examples. The oldest preserved evidence is not necessarily the exact origin of life.", "Photosynthesis increased oxygen; ozone protection and changing cell organization helped create opportunities for later life. The source places sexual reproduction after about 1.5 billion years ago.", "The <term>Cambrian explosion</term>, beginning around the course value of 541 Ma, was a geologically rapid diversification over millions of years, including many shelled organisms. It was not a single instantaneous event.", "Early Paleozoic seas included trilobites, corals, crinoids, and mollusks. Fish and reefs expanded, followed by plants and insects on land and later amphibian and reptile diversification."]], ["The end-Permian crisis", ["The <term>Great Dying</term> at about 252 Ma eliminated a large fraction of marine and terrestrial species. Its scale distinguishes it from ordinary background turnover.", "The source discusses Siberian volcanism, environmental effects of Pangaea, ocean anoxia or circulation changes, and a disputed impact proposal. These are causal hypotheses with different support, not equally established facts.", "When reconstructing a crisis, separate dated coincidence, a plausible mechanism, and direct evidence. Do not turn a listed possibility into a confirmed sole cause."]]], "questions": [["What does an ancient stromatolite most directly document?", ["A structure built through microbial activity", "The exact time life first originated", "The presence of abundant shelled animals", "The onset of widespread terrestrial forests"], "Stromatolites preserve evidence of microbial activity, not an exact origin date.", ["Preservation of a stromatolite establishes life by that time, not its first origin.", "Stromatolites document microbes rather than requiring abundant shelled animals.", "Microbial structures do not establish terrestrial forests."]], ["Which description best fits the Cambrian explosion?", ["Rapid diversification over millions of years", "The first origin of all microbial life", "The disappearance of most shelled organisms", "A slow event spanning all Precambrian time"], "It is geologically rapid diversification, not instantaneous creation of all life.", ["Microbial life predates the Cambrian diversification by billions of years.", "The event includes diversification of shelled groups rather than their general disappearance.", "The named event occupies a relatively limited interval near the start of the Cambrian, not all Precambrian time."]]], "writing": ["Why should a proposed cause of the end-Permian extinction not be accepted solely because it appears in a list?", "A proposed cause needs timing, mechanism, and evidence. A list includes hypotheses with unequal support, so inclusion alone does not establish causation."], "rubric": ["States that appearing in a list makes a cause a proposal, not a demonstrated conclusion.", "Requires a compatible event date and a plausible mechanism.", "Requires independent supporting evidence and recognizes that the listed hypotheses have unequal support."]}, {"n": 12, "title": "Later milestones: Mesozoic and Cenozoic", "pages": [2, 5], "groups": [["Life before the end-Cretaceous boundary", ["Triassic ecosystems included early mammals, dinosaur relatives, conifers, and marine ammonites. Mammals did not originate only after dinosaurs disappeared.", "Jurassic ecosystems included diverse dinosaurs and early birds such as Archaeopteryx. Cretaceous ecosystems included flowering plants, birds, mammals, marine reptiles, fish, and plankton.", "Dinosaur hip categories describe anatomy; a group name alone does not establish bird ancestry. Read the relationship described by the source instead of treating a label as a family tree."]], ["Extinction and later diversification", ["At about 66 Ma the end-Cretaceous crisis eliminated nonavian dinosaurs and many other groups. The Chicxulub impact in Mexico supplied dust and gases capable of disrupting sunlight and food webs.", "The chapter also discusses Deccan volcanism as a possible contributing influence. Distinguish an impact mechanism from an unsupported claim that every extinction had one identical cause.", "Mammals diversified extensively in the <term>Cenozoic</term>. Later ice-age ecosystems included large mammals; climate and other changing conditions are relevant to their losses.", "Origin, diversification, and extinction are separate milestones. A group can exist long before it becomes ecologically prominent."]]], "questions": [["Which statement correctly places mammals in geologic history?", ["They existed in the Mesozoic and diversified later", "They originated only after the end-Cretaceous extinction", "They diversified first and originated in the Cenozoic", "They disappeared entirely at the end-Cretaceous boundary"], "Early mammals preceded the end-Cretaceous extinction and later diversified.", ["The supplied Triassic record includes mammals before that extinction.", "Origin must precede diversification; the source places mammal origin earlier than the Cenozoic.", "Mammals survived the boundary and subsequently diversified."]], ["How could an impact disrupt ecosystems far from its crater?", ["Atmospheric dust and gases reduce sunlight and disrupt food webs", "The direct blast alone excavates every distant habitat", "Burial of the crater immediately ends all global effects", "Atmospheric particles increase sunlight at the surface"], "Atmospheric effects can propagate beyond the region of direct impact.", ["Direct excavation is local; atmospheric effects explain far-reaching disruption.", "Burial of the crater does not immediately remove atmospheric particles or restore food webs.", "Light-blocking particles reduce incoming sunlight rather than increase it."]]], "writing": ["Distinguish the origin of a group from its later ecological diversification using mammals.", "Mammals originated before the Cenozoic. After the end-Cretaceous crisis they expanded into many forms and ecological roles, so later diversification is not their first appearance."], "rubric": ["Places mammal origin before the Cenozoic.", "Describes later expansion in forms or ecological roles after the end-Cretaceous crisis.", "Explicitly distinguishes origin from diversification."]}, {"n": 13, "title": "Reconstruct histories from multiple clues", "pages": [2, 6], "groups": [["Correlate with independent evidence", ["Traceable lateral continuity is strong evidence that separated exposures belong to one unit. Distinctive beds, repeated sequences, shared events, and regional unconformities also support correlation.", "<term>Facies</term> can change sideways: a sandy beach and offshore mud may form at the same time. Equal age does not require identical lithology or thickness.", "Fossil assemblages, suitable isotope ages, and paleomagnetic reversal patterns add constraints. A matching pattern suggests correlation but must be evaluated with the rest of the evidence."]], ["Build a sequence, not a guess", ["Missing units may result from nondeposition, environmental restriction, thickness changes, or erosion. Inspect contacts to distinguish these alternatives.", "The supplied block combines old metamorphic basement, granite, unconformities, sedimentary beds, a dike and lava, fault motion, canyon cutting, and river gravel. Apply each visible relationship to a pair of events first.", "A fault can move more than once. A preexisting scarp and later displacement of lava can represent different episodes; “the fault” need not be one instant.", "Keep an event uncertain if no observed relation orders it. A complete-looking list is less useful than an honest set of supported constraints."]]], "questions": [["Sandstone grades laterally into offshore mudstone. What is a reasonable interpretation?", ["Different environments deposited contemporaneous facies", "The mudstone must predate every sandstone layer", "Different lithologies always represent different ages", "Only units of identical thickness can share an age"], "Lateral facies change can represent different settings at the same time.", ["A lateral facies change does not require every mudstone to be older than every sandstone.", "Contemporaneous environments can deposit different rock types.", "Lateral changes in deposition can produce different thicknesses in units of the same age."]], ["A fault scarp existed before lava erupted, and the lava is also offset. What history fits both observations?", ["Fault movement occurred before and after the eruption", "The lava must be older than every fault movement", "The scarp proves the lava was never displaced", "Only one instantaneous fault event is possible"], "Separate movements can bracket the eruption.", ["Offset lava records movement after eruption in addition to the earlier scarp.", "An earlier scarp does not cancel the observed later displacement of the lava.", "The two observations require movement on both sides of the eruption, which a single instantaneous event cannot provide."]]], "writing": ["Why should thickness differences not automatically prevent correlation of two fossil-bearing sections?", "Deposition rates, accommodation, facies, and erosion can vary laterally. Shared fossil and stratigraphic evidence may still correlate units of different thickness."], "rubric": ["Names at least one process that changes thickness laterally, such as deposition rate, accommodation, facies, or erosion.", "Explains that thickness is not itself a unique age indicator.", "Uses matching fossils or stratigraphic relations to support correlation despite thickness differences."]}, {"n": 14, "title": "Why geologic history matters", "pages": [2, 7], "groups": [["Hazards from a longer record", ["Lava superposition, weathering, and suitable isotopic dates reconstruct eruption history. This constrains hazards without predicting the exact date of the next eruption.", "Offset dated rocks constrain fault activity; a lava covering a scarp can limit older movement. Scarp preservation also depends on erosion.", "Flood gauges often cover a much shorter interval than deposits, terraces, soils, and datable charcoal. Geological evidence extends the history beyond human measurements."]], ["Resources and human history", ["Intrusions and crosscutting hydrothermal veins help reconstruct mineralization. The timing of folding, burial, migration, and erosion matters to oil and gas traps.", "Basin stratigraphy controls groundwater pathways. An isotope-based water residence time is a different measurement from the age of the surrounding sediment.", "Archaeology combines layer order, construction relationships, tree rings, and appropriate radiocarbon dates. Reused building stones can predate the wall containing them.", "Ancient hominid deposits can be bracketed by volcanic ash; carbon-14 is not a suitable clock for material millions of years old."]]], "questions": [["A groundwater sample has a residence age of 2,000 years in sandstone deposited 80 Ma ago. What follows?", ["The water and host rock record different events", "The sandstone must be only 2,000 years old", "One date must be wrong solely because they differ", "The water must have remained there for 80 Ma"], "Water residence and sediment deposition are distinct histories.", ["Sediment deposition and water residence are different events, so the water age does not redate the sandstone.", "Different event ages can both be valid; the numerical difference alone is not a contradiction.", "The measured residence history concerns the water, not the entire existence of its host rock."]], ["Why examine old flood deposits when a river has a century of gauge records?", ["Deposits can reveal floods beyond the measured interval", "A century necessarily samples the largest possible flood", "Terrace height alone gives next year’s flood date", "Flood deposits must all lie within the gauge interval"], "Geologic evidence can extend the hazard record beyond instrumentation.", ["A century may omit rare events documented in older deposits.", "Terrace geometry helps reconstruct past floods, not an exact future flood date.", "Deposits can survive from before gauge measurements began."]]], "writing": ["Explain why the age of stone reused in a wall need not date construction of that wall.", "The stone existed before it was reused. Its formation or earlier use provides an older limit, while the wall’s construction is a separate younger event."], "rubric": ["States that the reused stone existed before construction of the later wall.", "Identifies stone formation or earlier use as a distinct event from wall construction.", "Uses that older history as a limit rather than an exact date for the wall."]}, {"n": 15, "title": "Grand Canyon: a discontinuous history", "pages": [1, 3], "groups": [["Old rocks, younger gaps, much younger canyon", ["Basement metamorphic rocks formed about 1.7 Ga, with somewhat younger granite. Uplift and erosion exposed this deep crust before later sediment accumulated.", "Late Precambrian strata around 1.1 Ga were deposited, tilted, and eroded. Younger Paleozoic strata lie across the resulting angular unconformity.", "Contacts above crystalline basement may be nonconformities; contacts above tilted layered rocks may be angular unconformities. Parallel sedimentary intervals also contain disconformities.", "Paleozoic deposits record changing marine, river, and dune environments. Missing Ordovician and Silurian record and other gaps prevent treating the exposed column as continuous time."]], ["Bracket the canyon separately", ["The chapter describes much later deformation and uplift, followed by major canyon excavation chiefly during the last roughly 5 million years. This is its reconstruction, not the age of the wall rocks.", "An approximately 8 Ma plateau lava predates the main canyon; lava entering an existing canyon around 4–5 Ma shows the canyon already existed then. Faults also offset much younger basalt.", "The canyon preserves only parts of Earth history: the earliest billions of years and long later intervals are absent. Rock thickness is not a uniform clock."]]], "questions": [["A lava flowed down into an already existing canyon. Which age relation is secure?", ["The canyon existed before that lava flow", "The lava predates all canyon excavation", "The canyon and basement formed together", "The lava dates every canyon wall layer"], "A flow entering a canyon requires the canyon to be present first.", ["The lava entered a canyon that had already been excavated.", "Basement formed long before later canyon erosion.", "The lava dates its own eruption, not each older unit exposed in the walls."]], ["Why can a Grand Canyon rock column omit a long span of time between adjacent layers?", ["Erosion or nondeposition created an unconformity", "A thinner bed necessarily records a shorter time", "Adjacent layers must have continuously accumulated", "Parallel bedding always rules out missing time"], "Unconformities represent missing record, not necessarily large visible spacing.", ["Thickness also depends on deposition rate, erosion, and gaps, not just duration.", "Adjacent beds can be separated by an interval of erosion or nondeposition.", "A disconformity can separate parallel sedimentary beds."]]], "writing": ["Separate the age of Grand Canyon basement, its sedimentary cover, and canyon incision.", "Basement formed first in deep time; younger sediments accumulated after exposure and across gaps; much later uplift and erosion cut the canyon through the older rock record."], "rubric": ["Places basement formation first.", "Places sedimentary deposition later, recognizing intervening exposure or missing time.", "Places canyon incision much later than both basement and sedimentary cover."]}, {"n": 16, "title": "Investigation: use every independent constraint", "pages": [1, 4], "groups": [["Read the field notes before ordering labels", ["In the supplied investigation, C is Cambrian beach sandstone/conglomerate, B is Ordovician marine shale, A is Permian terrestrial sandstone, S is Jurassic river/lake sandstone, and K is Cretaceous marine limestone. Fossils establish their relative sequence.", "Granite G is weathered beneath C, and C contains G clasts: G is older and the depositional contact is a <term>nonconformity</term>. The eroded parallel B–A contact is a <term>disconformity</term>.", "Basalt dike D bakes and cuts A, B, C, and G, so it is younger than those units. Fault F cuts several sedimentary units locally and in nearby exposures; include the field notes beyond the block view.", "River gravel R has a developed soil and middle-Cenozoic mammals. Its former channel is truncated by canyon N, and lava L enters that canyon: R deposition precedes canyon cutting, which precedes that lava."]], ["Compute ages under stated assumptions", ["For G, 125 parent atoms and 875 daughter atoms give a parent fraction of 1/8. Under the simple closed-system, zero-initial-daughter model, that is three 500-million-year half-lives, or <term>1,500 Ma</term>.", "For D, equal parent and daughter amounts give one half-life under the same model. With a 40-million-year half-life, the calculated age is <term>40 Ma</term>.", "These are instructional calculations, not measured analytical certainty. Separate numerical constraints, fossil ordering, and visible crosscutting evidence; do not infer an unsupported order merely from alphabetical labels."]]], "questions": [["Under a zero-initial-daughter closed-system model, a granite has 125 parent and 875 daughter atoms. Its half-life is 500 Ma. What age follows?", ["1,500 Ma", "500 Ma", "1,000 Ma", "4,000 Ma"], "125/1000 is 1/8, requiring three half-lives of 500 Ma each.", ["500 Ma is one half-life and would leave half of the initial parent.", "1,000 Ma is two half-lives and would leave one-quarter.", "4,000 Ma is eight half-lives; the observed fraction requires only three."]], ["In the investigation, C contains granite G clasts and rests on its weathered surface. Which interpretation follows?", ["C was deposited after erosion exposed G", "G intruded C after C was deposited", "C and G must have identical crystallization ages", "C is older because its fossils are marine"], "The granite existed before clast production and deposition of the overlying sediment.", ["The weathered surface and granite clasts show deposition on preexisting granite, not later granite intrusion.", "Clast incorporation requires the granite to predate the deposit.", "Marine fossils constrain the sediment but do not make it older than the granite clasts it contains."]]], "writing": ["Use the supplied R, N, and L relationships to describe three events in order.", "River gravel R accumulated in an older channel; canyon N later truncated that channel; lava L subsequently flowed into the already existing canyon."], "rubric": ["Places deposition of river gravel R first.", "Places canyon N cutting and truncating the older channel second.", "Places lava L entering the already existing canyon last."]}];
